WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
How can the Bay's crab estimate jump when the Bay has not grown?

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.200
Watch a survey boat cross the winter water.

3
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.000
Its dredge catches some crabs buried in the mud.

4
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.860
Those catches become an estimate for the whole Bay.

5
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.380
A moving estimate does not require a moving shoreline.

6
00:00:22.783 --> 00:00:25.243
The earlier estimate appears as a bar.

7
00:00:26.423 --> 00:00:29.063
The next estimate appears beside it, much taller.

8
00:00:30.423 --> 00:00:32.323
These are estimates, not exact counts.

9
00:00:33.543 --> 00:00:36.943
The survey pattern is real, but it does not tell us the cause.

10
00:00:38.263 --> 00:00:40.703
So read height before hunting for explanations.

11
00:00:42.017 --> 00:00:45.037
To explain the change, begin with a population.

12
00:00:46.477 --> 00:00:50.917
A population is every member of the same species living in the same place.

13
00:00:52.197 --> 00:00:54.297
Put the Bay's crabs inside one window.

14
00:00:55.637 --> 00:00:57.897
Now track what enters and leaves that window.

15
00:00:59.077 --> 00:01:01.897
The boundary matters because movement crosses it.

16
00:01:01.897 --> 00:01:02.867
To explain the change, begin with the bays crabs inside one window.

17
00:01:02.867 --> 00:01:05.167
Births add crabs inside the window.

18
00:01:06.607 --> 00:01:08.147
Arrivals add crabs moving in.

19
00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:10.567
Deaths remove crabs.

20
00:01:11.807 --> 00:01:13.687
Departures remove crabs moving out.

21
00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:16.727
Call births and arrivals additions.

22
00:01:18.107 --> 00:01:19.967
Call deaths and departures losses.

23
00:01:21.807 --> 00:01:24.247
Population change is the balance between them.

24
00:01:25.407 --> 00:01:29.567
Additions and losses can occur at the same time while the population changes.

25
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:35.390
Imagine the loss arrows getting wider while the addition arrows stay unchanged.

26
00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:38.990
What should happen to the number of crabs inside?

27
00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.150
Follow the arrows before you decide.

28
00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.510
Watch the balance, not any single dot.

29
00:01:51.483 --> 00:01:54.783
The number falls because losses now exceed additions.

30
00:01:56.263 --> 00:01:58.383
Reverse the balance, and the number rises.

31
00:01:59.683 --> 00:02:02.063
Match the arrows, and the number stays steady.

32
00:02:03.303 --> 00:02:08.783
This gives the rule: populations rise when additions exceed losses, and fall when

33
00:02:08.783 --> 00:02:10.103
losses exceed additions.

34
00:02:11.443 --> 00:02:13.483
The dots show direction, not a forecast.

35
00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:18.387
A limiting factor changes at least one addition or loss.

36
00:02:19.707 --> 00:02:21.767
A very cold winter can increase deaths.

37
00:02:23.127 --> 00:02:25.787
Better shelter can lower deaths among young crabs.

38
00:02:27.027 --> 00:02:30.027
Heavy harvest increases losses caused by people.

39
00:02:31.307 --> 00:02:33.347
Each factor matters through the same balance.

40
00:02:34.627 --> 00:02:36.727
Different factors can push the same arrow.

41
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.640
The balance tells whether a population rises or falls over a chosen time.

42
00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:45.960
It cannot identify a cause by itself.

43
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:52.140
Scientists need measurements of cold, shelter, predators, harvest, and other

44
00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.380
factors. The survey gives the pattern.

45
00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:58.920
Evidence about the arrows tests explanations.

46
00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.900
That extra evidence separates a pattern from a tested cause.

47
00:03:04.983 --> 00:03:06.623
Return to the winter bars.

48
00:03:07.863 --> 00:03:12.403
If the estimates are close to the truth, their jump means additions beat losses

49
00:03:12.403 --> 00:03:13.403
during the interval.

50
00:03:14.503 --> 00:03:16.263
The bars alone cannot say why.

51
00:03:17.523 --> 00:03:18.863
Keep the Bay window in mind.

52
00:03:20.163 --> 00:03:24.603
Every explanation must show which addition or loss changed, and what evidence

53
00:03:24.603 --> 00:03:25.403
supports it.
